{
  "case_id": "2006-06-593",
  "document_hash": "96b6bcd94461be0ff06162a80095bf1ead63d7d7e50d895ecbd7b198103d713e",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2006-06-593",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "96b6bcd94461be0ff06162a80095bf1ead63d7d7e50d895ecbd7b198103d713e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-96b6bcd94461-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In the 1974 amendments to the Fair Labor Standards Act, Congress made one thing very clear, that it wanted the Department of Labor to define the boundaries and fill in the details of the companionship services exemption. And I think that has two important implications for this case.\nFirst of all, when the Department has filled in the details, after notice and comment rulemaking, its regulations should receive Chevron deference as long as they are permissible implementation of the statute.\nSecond, and particularly specific to this case, if there are ambiguities in the regulations, or as we have here, an apparent facial inconsistency, the court should accept the Secretary's resolution of that ambiguity provided that it is a reasonable one."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-06-593",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "96b6bcd94461be0ff06162a80095bf1ead63d7d7e50d895ecbd7b198103d713e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-96b6bcd94461-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: I thought the words were home of the person who employs, not who receives the services but who employs."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-06-593",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "96b6bcd94461be0ff06162a80095bf1ead63d7d7e50d895ecbd7b198103d713e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-96b6bcd94461-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Mr. Salmons, can I ask you a question about the importance of the whole litigation. Am I correct in believing that there's a provision in the law that protects the defendants from damages liability if they relied in good faith on the regulation, so that what we're really talking about is whether the regulation would apply in the future rather than there being a damage issue in the case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2006-06-593",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "96b6bcd94461be0ff06162a80095bf1ead63d7d7e50d895ecbd7b198103d713e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-96b6bcd94461-0001-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SALMONS: Well, there is a safe harbor provision that allows for reliance by employers on a statement by the agency."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-06-593",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "96b6bcd94461be0ff06162a80095bf1ead63d7d7e50d895ecbd7b198103d713e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-96b6bcd94461-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: So in your view we're really faced with a question of whether the regulation should be given prospective effect."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2006-06-593",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "96b6bcd94461be0ff06162a80095bf1ead63d7d7e50d895ecbd7b198103d713e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-96b6bcd94461-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Not seemingly conflicted. They conflict."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-06-593",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "96b6bcd94461be0ff06162a80095bf1ead63d7d7e50d895ecbd7b198103d713e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-96b6bcd94461-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "WHERE IS THE PROPOSITION THAT AN INTERPRETIVE REGULATION IS SOMEHOW NOT A FULL-FLEDGED BINDING REGULATION?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Becker",
      "case_id": "2006-06-593",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "96b6bcd94461be0ff06162a80095bf1ead63d7d7e50d895ecbd7b198103d713e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-96b6bcd94461-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The important difference here, Justice Scalia, is the statute. The statute, like the tax statute which was interpreted by this Court in Vogel and Rowan, creates two types of authority."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Becker",
      "case_id": "2006-06-593",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "96b6bcd94461be0ff06162a80095bf1ead63d7d7e50d895ecbd7b198103d713e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-96b6bcd94461-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We are relying on a very clear statement both in the regulations, 552.2(c), as well as in both the proposed regulations and the final regulations, which clearly state that only those in Part A define and delimit."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-06-593",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "96b6bcd94461be0ff06162a80095bf1ead63d7d7e50d895ecbd7b198103d713e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-96b6bcd94461-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You mean we're going to divide all administrative law now into those -- those regulations -- those provisions that were adopted by Congress pre-Chevron and those adopted by Congress post-Chevron, and for the ones adopted pre-Chevron we're going to treat regulation as essentially suggestions by the agency which we give Skidmore deference to, and the ones after Chevron, we're going to treat differently."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2006-06-593",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "96b6bcd94461be0ff06162a80095bf1ead63d7d7e50d895ecbd7b198103d713e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-96b6bcd94461-0002-0005",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
